Container storage is ephemeral; data written inside a container layer is deleted 
when the container restarts. Kubernetes decouples storage management into 
three distinct layers:

+-----------------------------------------------------------+
|                    StorageClass (SC)                      |  Blueprint defining the dynamic provisioner
|        (e.g., k8s.io/minikube-hostpath, AWS gp3)          |  (Creates actual storage on demand)
+-----------------------------+-----------------------------+
                              |
                              v  Dynamically provisions
+-----------------------------------------------------------+
|                  PersistentVolume (PV)                    |  Cluster-wide storage volume allocated on the 
|               Capacity: 500Mi, Access: RWO                |  underlying disk or cloud provider
+-----------------------------+-----------------------------+
                              |
                              v  Binds 1:1
+-----------------------------------------------------------+
|               PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC)                 |  Namespace-scoped user request for storage size
|             web-data-pvc (Requests: 500Mi)                |  and access modes
+-----------------------------+-----------------------------+
                              |
                              v  Mounted via volumeMounts
+-----------------------------------------------------------+
|                      Pod Deployment                       |  Application consumes storage via a mount path
|           mountPath: /usr/share/nginx/html                |  like a local directory
+-----------------------------------------------------------+


Volume Access Modes
ReadWriteOnce (RWO): Volume can be mounted as read-write by a single node at a time (standard block storage, EBS, hostpath).

ReadOnlyMany (ROX): Volume can be mounted read-only by many nodes concurrently.

ReadWriteMany (RWX): Volume can be mounted read-write by multiple nodes simultaneously (network filesystems like NFS, AWS EFS).

ReadWriteOncePod (RWOP): Volume can be mounted as read-write by a single Pod across the entire cluster.

